using System.Collections.Generic;

/// res://scripts/DefaultMaze.cs — the built-in "Straight Shot" maze every
/// player has access to online, regardless of saved slots.
///
/// Owner: "make it so everyone has access to a default maze which is just a
/// straight line... as an option in online games." This is a CODE-DEFINED
/// maze, not a save slot: built fresh in memory every time it's needed, never
/// written under %APPDATA%, never occupies one of MazeSerializer's slots.
/// Wherever a real slot index would normally flow (OnlineGameState.
/// SelectedMazeSlot, the "maze" relay payload, MazeSerializer.RecordMatchOutcome),
/// this maze is identified by SentinelSlot instead — see PlayGameScreen.cs /
/// PreGameLobby.cs / OnlineDungeonArena.cs for the explicit handling at each
/// of those points.
public static class DefaultMaze
{
    /// Sentinel "slot" for the built-in maze — never a real MazeSerializer slot
    /// (those are 0..MazeSerializer.SlotCount-1, always >= 0). Reuses the
    /// literal the picker's old "no mazes / disabled" placeholder item used to
    /// use for the same id: that placeholder is only ever added when the
    /// dropdown would otherwise be completely EMPTY (see PopulateMazeDropdown
    /// in both pickers), and this maze being unconditionally present as the
    /// first entry means the dropdown is never empty any more — so that old
    /// branch can no longer execute, and there is no actual collision.
    public const int SentinelSlot = -1;

    public const string DisplayName = "Straight Shot (default)";

    /// Row count of the straight run between Start and Exit. Sits on the same
    /// 10-row grid convention the editor and DungeonArena.FlipMazeZ's hardcoded
    /// GridH both assume (Start at row 0, Exit at row GridH-1) — this maze
    /// never touches the editor, but the online arena's maze-B flip math does
    /// still hardcode a 10-row grid, so this shape has to fit it exactly
    /// rather than picking an arbitrary length.
    const int GridH = 10;
    const int Column = 4;

    /// Builds a fresh MazeData: Start at (4,0), eight Straight pieces filling
    /// rows 1-8, Exit at (4,9) — all on floor 0, all default rotation (Start
    /// opens S, Straight opens N|S, Exit opens N — PieceDB's base openings
    /// already point every piece into the next one with no rotation needed).
    /// Long enough to actually get a sprint/bhop chain going, short enough to
    /// stay a trivial "everyone has SOMETHING to play" fallback.
    public static MazeData Build()
    {
        var pieces = new List<MazePiece>
        {
            new MazePiece { Type = PieceType.Start, X = Column, Y = 0, Floor = 0 },
        };
        for (int y = 1; y < GridH - 1; y++)
            pieces.Add(new MazePiece { Type = PieceType.Straight, X = Column, Y = y, Floor = 0 });
        pieces.Add(new MazePiece { Type = PieceType.Exit, X = Column, Y = GridH - 1, Floor = 0 });

        return new MazeData
        {
            Name        = DisplayName,
            Pieces      = pieces,
            IsOnline    = true,
            IsGameReady = true,
        };
    }
}
